Priorities moved and it is not queued behind anything, so a comment on 97
retracts that sentence -- a promise nobody intends to keep parks the defect
under our name rather than theirs. The fix-location argument is untouched and
was always the half worth more: the recursive-sum helper is synthesised in
`Emit/X86_64.codex`, below the wire, so arm64 answers `ne`, riscv is unmeasured
and the zig plug compares pointers; hoisting it into lowering beside
`deriving Eq` fixes all three at once. The comment carries one witness we did
not have when we filed -- over 326 corpus programs compiled AND run,
`recursive-eq` is the run's single `differ`. `findings/README.md` and
`TYPEDEF-PRUNE.md` both claimed we owed this; both corrected.

**`bank_truth.py` now checks the tree, not just the seed.** The ERGONOMICS item
from this morning is closed. A bank's name comes from the seed and the seed
does not identify a checkout: `seed/Codex.cdx` is tracked upstream and a PR
against the compiler source does not rebuild it, so `master-plus-outbound`
carries u53's seed byte for byte and would have banked as `u53`, over the
release bank, with nothing refusing it.

`seed_identity.tree_stamp()` supplies the missing half, and **the release
commit is derived rather than tabulated**: the seed only changes when a release
rebuilds it, so the newest commit reachable from HEAD that touched
`seed/Codex.cdx` IS the release this tree descends from, and HEAD must be it.
That needs no teaching by a new release form, which is `update_label`'s
recurring failure -- twice now. The gate fires only where a name can collide;
an unreleased seed already banks as `seed-<hash>`. `--force` still gets
through and must TYPE a reason, which lands in the new `TREE` file beside
`SEED` (head, branch, release commit, verdict); a `--force` with no reason is
the silent overwrite wearing a flag, and is refused.

**Both arms shown to fire**, `tree_stamp` taking a rev so the refusal can be
exercised without checking anything out:

    u53-rebank (HEAD)          release 58b08c38  HEAD is it     -> proceeds
    master-plus-outbound       release 58b08c38  HEAD IS NOT IT -> refuses
    same, --force, no reason                                    -> refuses

The middle row is the run that would have overwritten `truth/u53` this morning.
`truth/u53` byte-identical after all three probes. `codex_branch()` moved from
`bank_golds.py` into `seed_identity.py` on the way, `bank_truth` being its
second caller.

## 2026-09-01 afternoon -- the Rust front end starts, and apps/ leaves the corpus

**`rust-codex-compiler` exists**, at `~/showell_repos/rust-codex-compiler`,
pushed to `github.com/showell/rust-codex-compiler` over the DEFAULT ssh key --
the `github-ladder` and `github-nr` aliases are deploy keys scoped to their own
repos and are refused there.

**A day-zero find that would have cost weeks: `char-code` is not ASCII.** It is
a private frequency-ordered alphabet -- 1 newline, 2 space, 3..12 the digits,
13..38 lowercase as `etaoinshrdlcumwfgypbvkjxqz`, 39..64 uppercase at
lowercase+26, 65..96 punctuation. So `cc-first-upper : Integer = char-code 'E'`
is CORRECT: 'E' is the lowest-coded uppercase letter and 'Z' the highest, and
the test is a range. `ChapterScoper`'s `c - 26` lowercasing is right too. The
reason it matters is that `char-code 'A'` is CONSTANT-FOLDED into the IR as
`(int-lit 41)`, so a front end folding it to 65 differs from the golds on every
program containing a character literal, with the diff far from its cause. New
`charcode_probe.py` derives the table from `native/codexir` in ~0.05s and
checks it structurally.

**Lexer: byte-identical to `lex.truth` on the first run**, 5,335 tokens. It is
LOSSLESS -- trivia is emitted -- which buys a gate needing no gold at all:
`concat(tokens) == source` over 2,695 files, 16.3 MB, in 0.147s.

**Parser: document layer, expressions and type expressions.** `parse.truth`
byte-identical. The compiler's own 64 chapters: 0 parse errors, 0 unread
bodies, 0 unread types, 4,858 definitions. Still flat and still owed before
desugar: 4,530 patterns, 286 type definitions, 49 act/trying/with blocks.

**Two gate lessons, both measured rather than reasoned.** A pure token-coverage
check is far too weak: a parser broken to stop consuming bodies passed it
untouched, the orphans reappearing as loose lines counted once each. The gate
is now coverage AND homelessness -- 367 loose tokens healthy, 357,339 broken.
And the scale gate is BLIND TO SHAPE: giving `+` and `*` equal precedence
leaves it entirely green while the shape tests fail. Neither `parse.truth` nor
`desugar.truth` inspects an expression, so shape rests on unit tests until the
IR golds.

**apps/ is out of the corpus for good, and the FLAG is gone rather than
re-defaulted.** `core` was already the default and bought nothing: today's run
passed `--scope all` explicitly, spent 55 minutes to reach 100 of 1,705 and was
killed. The corpus is 1,229 programs and `EXCLUDED_DIRS` is the only lever.
`compare_arms` loses `--scope` too, and the scope component of its run ids.

**The kill exposed a defect and a false promise.** `bank_golds.py` read
`transpile.json`, written once after the loop, while `transpile.jsonl` is
corpus_run's own crash-safe per-program record -- so an interrupted run banked
NOTHING, under a chain script whose status line promised to "bank what it
produced anyway". The promise was in the wrapper; the capability was never in
the tool. It now falls back to the `.jsonl` and marks the bank PARTIAL on
stdout and in its PROVENANCE. Running that new path rather than reading it --
BOX.md item 11 -- immediately caught a PROVENANCE line naming the flag that had
just been deleted.

`BOX.md` gains the general form as a Before item: **if this is killed halfway,
is the finished work still usable BY THE NEXT STAGE?** A chain is only as
resumable as its narrowest consumer, and a producer writing a perfect
incremental log buys nothing if the next step opens the end-of-run file.

**The Rust interpreter's corpus sweep, re-run and CLASSIFIED (2026-09-02).**
551 of 979 over master-plus-outbound, `codexrun sweep` at rust pin `4ba3d8f`
-- the same count as before that day's two speed rounds, which is the
attribution that they moved cost and not behaviour. The ranked summary was
misleading: it keys causes on the message text and the message carries a line
number, so one missing builtin read as six rows. Aggregated by cause CLASS the
428 failures are three populations: ~230 want an OS/hardware builtin
(`port-out-32`, `alloc-bytes`, `block-read-sector`, `poke-mmio-32`,
`process-spawn`) and are OUT OF THE INTERPRETER'S WORLD rather than failing in
it; 130 RAN and printed the wrong thing, which is the real list; the rest is a
short tail of genuine defects. Interpreter speed the same day: 9,142,789 ->
17,613,083 steps/sec, no quadratic anywhere -- `render` is 605,930,430 steps
at the same rate as `pond`'s 3,824.

**The arity guess, and an error that named the wrong builtin (2026-09-02).**
`keys-collision` failed as ``builtin `bit-and` is not interpreted yet (given a
function, an integer)`` with `bit-and` fully implemented. Cause:
`uefi-read-key-ex : Integer` is NULLARY, `builtins_probe.py` could not read a
bare type name (its search demanded a `(` after `Just`), so it and 22 others
fell down the undeclared path and took an arity of 0 by default -- which the
interpreter then read as "declares no type" and guessed 1 for, binding a
function where an Integer belonged. `ForAllEff` was not transparent either, so
`process-spawn` and three siblings read as taking no arguments. The probe now
refuses a type head it does not know rather than counting it 0 -- it fired on
its first run, on `s-new`'s `deck-record` -- and the table carries
`Option<usize>`, because `Some(0)` and `None` are different facts. Sweep 551 of
979 before and after, with the `ok` SET identical; ~34 programs moved their
error from a downstream victim to its cause. Ladder `1286102`, rust `d5cef6e`.
